 volume increases the self-weight of structural components, which affects the loads transferred to foundations and supporting elements, and can constrain geometric and material selection decisions. In applications where load-bearing capacity per unit cross-section is the primary criterion, higher density may accompany greater mechanical stiffness or strength for certain materials; however, the additional weight imposes design constraints in contexts where minimizing total mass is a priority. The net suitability of a high-density material in any structural role depends on the balance between mechanical performance requirements and permissible weight. [KB:metals.md]\n^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The density data is directly supported by metals.md. The discussion of structural significance draws on general engineering mechanics principles that are well established in the materials science literature; however, metals.md does not document stiffness, yield strength, or other mechanical properties of the metals, so the broader structural significance discussion extends beyond the direct KB evidence. No contradictions with KB data are present.]\n\n## SLOT 3: Iron versus Aluminum Comparison\n\nThe principal property differences between iron and aluminum, as documented in the knowledge base, are summarized below. [KB:metals.md]\n- **Melting point**: Iron melts at $1538\\ ^\\circ\\text{C}$; aluminum melts at $660\\ ^\\circ\\text{C}$, a difference of $878\\ ^\\circ\\text{C}$. Iron exhibits substantially greater thermal resistance under high-temperature conditions. [KB:metals.md]\n- **Density**: Iron has a density of $7.874\\ \\text{g/cm}^3$; aluminum has a density of $2.70\\ \\text{g/cm}^3$. Aluminum is approximately 2.9 times less dense, resulting in a substantially lower mass per unit volume. [KB:metals.md]\n- **Atomic number**: Iron has atomic number 26; aluminum has atomic number 13. This difference reflects their respective positions in the periodic table and their distinct electron shell configurations. [KB:metals.md]\n^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: All property values for iron and aluminum, including melting points, densities, and atomic numbers, are stated explicitly in metals.md. The computed differences, including the 878 C melting point gap and the approximately 2.9-fold density ratio (7.874 / 2.70 = 2.916), are arithmetic derivations from the stated values. No external sources were required, as metals.md fully addresses the scope of this comparison.]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
